    "body": "### Problem\n\nTyping the first `/` of a substitute command such as `:%s/...` freezes the UI if the last search pattern was a pathological regex. This happens before any new pattern is typed, because:\n\n1. An empty `:s` pattern reuses the last search pattern.\n2. Default setting `inccommand=nosplit` immediately triggers a live preview of the last pattern.\n3. Vim regex evaluation time is unbounded in this case.\n\nA prior search `/` or `:s` leaves a bad last pattern `@/`, then a normal `:%s/find/replace/g` freezes at the first `/`. The freeze can effectively hang until the process is killed.\n\nI hit this accidentally went a few months thinking nvim was broken by some IT antivirus software on my work computer. Admittedly I am not a heavy user so have not tried using it very much -- whenever it failed to work, I just used gvim or Notepad or Notepad++ instead.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Start with `nvim --clean`\n2. Enter text `aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa/bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb` on first line that will trigger a pathological pattern.\n3. Execute regular search command `/` for a pathological pattern like `.\\+\\/\\(.\\+\\)\\+ft\\/\\1`. In ~3 seconds it returns **E363: Pattern uses more memory than 'maxmempattern'.** and asks whether or not to continue. It's easy to interrupt and abort by pressing Ctrl+C three times (with status message updates given after each Ctrl+C). \n4. Start executing search-and-replace with `:%s/`, and notice UI freezes as soon as the first slash `/` is typed. On my system with this reproduction scenario, it freezes for ~13 seconds before the slash is typed and I can type something else. \n\n### Expected behavior\n\nA few alternate behaviors come to my mind:\n- Typing `:%s/` should remain responsive even if pattern matching is expensive. E.g. if typing `:%s/` freezes the UI, the system should recognize once the user types `:%s/a`, abort in-progress matching, and start a new matching process on `a` instead. \n- Search-and-replace should include time-limited errors similar to **E363: Pattern uses more memory than 'maxmempattern'.** that are triggered by regular search. If aborted while processing the most recent match, the most recent match should be updated to empty.",
